=== Client-side vs Server-side wiki page ===
A sidebar application can be either one of the following types by where the resources are located;
### Client side applications
- Resources (html, js, css, images) are available in the extension package
- Ajax calls can be used to fetch data from the server
- `manifest.json` sample;
```javascript
{
  ...
  "sidebar_action": {
    "default_icon": {                      
      "16": "images/icon16.png"
    },
    "default_page": "index.html",          
    "default_title": "Client side application"
  }
  ...
}
```
- Typical example of client side application is the widget panel in Whale browser sidebar. All of the UI resources are located in the extension package and some widgets fetch data from the server (e.g. exchange rate) and updates the UI.
- In this case, the local scripts have privilege to use the [extension APIs](https://developer.chrome.com/extensions/manifest).

![](https://github.com/naver/whale-developers/blob/master/docs/NCapture004.PNG?raw=true)

### Server side application
- If you are running a mobile web service, you may want to reuse it and build a package as a sidebar extension.
- However, you still need a index page to initiate your sidebar extension and the `manifest.json` should look like following;
```javascript
{
  ...
  "sidebar_action": {
    "default_icon": {                      
      "16": "images/icon16.png"
    },
    "default_page": "index.html",          
    "default_title": "Client side application",
    "use_navigation_bar": true,
    "mobile_user_agent": true
  }
  ...
}
```
- You can use the navigation bar if you need setting the `use_navigation_bar` the true.

![](https://github.com/naver/whale-developers/raw/master/docs/253a55b2-0fc8-11e7-9b7d-4afddeff04f8.png)
- Your `index.html` may look like this which redirects your extension to your website;
```
<html>
<head>
  <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0;URL='https://YOURWEBSITE.com'">
</head>
<body>
</body
</html>
```
- Or else you can also create a javascript file to replace the index page, if you do not want to create history.

index.html
```
<html>
<head>
  <script src="index.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
</body
</html>
```
index.js
```
location.replace('https://YOURWEBSITE.com');
```
- The advantage of wrapping your mobile web as a sidebar extension, rather than just adding your mobile web as a web panel, is that you can inject all the powerful extension APIs in your mobile service. Some examples are;
  * Add badges to you sidebar icon, when you have updates in your extension using [whale.sidebarAction.setIcon](https://github.com/naver/whale-developers/wiki/whale.sidebarAction)
  * Send text selections form web content to sidebar extensions using [chrome.contextMenus]
(https://developer.chrome.com/extensions/contextMenus)
![](https://github.com/naver/whale-developers/blob/master/docs/contextmenu.png?raw=true)
  * Open sidebar from `background.js` when you want to alert the user using [whale.sidebarAction.show](https://github.com/naver/whale-developers/wiki/whale.sidebarAction)
  * And so on...
- Typical example of server side application is the [Sidebar - Music Player](https://store.whale.naver.com/detail/fgjhgmbkbffhjogahkckmbfhnjpaiaed). The player resources are loaded from the web, and some extra UI are injected using [content scripts](https://developer.chrome.com/extensions/content_scripts).

![](https://github.com/naver/whale-developers/blob/master/docs/NCapture005.PNG?raw=true)

> You can also toggle you application between client side pages and server side pages.
### When you want to move from local file to web, you can either use one of the following;
* `<a href="http://MYWEBSITE.com" target="_self">Link from local page to website</a>`
* using javascript `location.href="http://MYWEBSITE.com";`
* using extension api `whale.sidebarAction.show({ url:'http://MYWEBSITE.com' })`
### When you want to come back to local file, the only way is;
* using extension api `whale.sidebarAction.show({ url: whale.runtime.getURL('index.html') })`
* You must keep in mind that [content scripts](https://developer.chrome.com/extensions/content_scripts) do not have access to extension APIs. You can still send messages to [background pages](https://developer.chrome.com/extensions/background_pages) using [message passing](https://developer.chrome.com/extensions/messaging) in order to invoke extension APIs from web contents. Same scripts are;

content_script.js
```
whale.runtime.sendMessage({
   'action': 'openLocalPage'
}, function(response) {

});
```

background.js
```
whale.runtime.onMessage.addListener(function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
  if (request.action === 'openLocalPage') {
    whale.sidebarAction.show({ 
      url: whale.runtime.getURL('index.html') 
    };
  }
});

```
